sentences.t:
Ayudól el Criador, el Señor que es en çielo.
Él con todo esto priso a Murviedro;
ya vi[d]íe mio Çid que Dios le iva valiendo.

sentences.l:
Helped-him the Creator, the Lord who is in heaven.
He with all this took Murviedro;
now saw my Çid that God him was aiding.

sentences.t:
En sus tierras somos e femosles tod mal,
bevemos so vino e comemos el so pan;
si nos çercar vienen, con derecho lo fazen.

sentences.l:
In their lands we-are and we-make-to-them all harm,
we-drink their wine and we-eat the their bread;
if us to-besiege they-come, with right it they-do.

sentences.t:
Los de Valençia çercados nos han;
si en estas tierras quisiéremos durar,
firme mientre son estos a escarmentar.

sentences.l:
Those of Valençia besieged us have;
if in these lands we-should-wish to-last,
firm-ly are these to punish.

sentences.t:
Passe la noche e venga la mañana,
aparejados me se[e]d a cavallos e armas;
iremos ve[e]r aquella su almofalla.

sentences.l:
Let-pass the night and let-come the morning,
ready for-me be with horses and arms;
we-will-go to-see that their army.

sentences.t:
Del otra part entróles Álbar Fáñez;
maguer les pesa oviéronse a dar e a arrancar:
de pie[de]s de cavallo los ques pudieron escapar.

sentences.l:
From-the other side entered-them Álbar Fáñez;
though to-them it-weighs they-had-themselves to give and to flee:
by feet of horse those who-themselves could escape.

sentences.t:
Grandes son las ganançias que mio Çid fechas ha;
robavan el campo e piénssanse de tornar.
Entravan a Murviedro con estas ganançias que traen;
grand es el gozo que va por es logar.

sentences.l:
Great are the gains that my Çid made has;
they-were-plundering the field and they-think-themselves to return.
They-were-entering to Murviedro with these gains that they-bring;
great is the joy that goes through that place.

sentences.t:
Prisieron Çebolla e quanto que es i adelant;
miedo an en Valençia que no saben qué se far;
las nuevas de mio Çid, sabet, sonando van.

sentences.l:
They-took Çebolla and as-much that is there onward;
fear they-have in Valençia that not they-know what themselves to-do;
the tidings of my Çid, know-ye, sounding go.
